  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "This lesson is about one of the most useful special rules in the H.C.F. and L.C.M. chapter: how to find the H.C.F. and L.C.M. when the numbers themselves are fractions.\n\nThe book gives two direct formulas. They look similar, but the numerator and denominator operations move in opposite directions.\n\nBy the end, you should be able to look at several fractions, identify which formula is needed, apply it to any number of fractions, and recognize the special 'least fraction divisible by each given fraction' pattern.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "fraction_big_picture"
        }
      ]
    },
    "fraction_big_picture": {
      "id": "fraction_big_picture",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Suppose we have fractions such as:\n\na/b, c/d and e/f\n\nFor H.C.F., the book tells us to take:\n\nH.C.F. of numerators ÷ L.C.M. of denominators\n\nFor L.C.M., the book tells us to take:\n\nL.C.M. of numerators ÷ H.C.F. of denominators\n\nThe key memory pattern is:\n\nH.C.F. → H.C.F. on top, L.C.M. below\nL.C.M. → L.C.M. on top, H.C.F. below\n\nNotice how the operation changes in the denominator.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "hcf_formula"
        }
      ]
    },
    "hcf_formula": {
      "id": "hcf_formula",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's write the first rule exactly in a form that is easy to use.\n\nFor fractions a/b, c/d and e/f:\n\nH.C.F. = H.C.F.(a, c, e) / L.C.M.(b, d, f)\n\nSo there are two separate jobs:\n\n1. Look only at the numerators and find their H.C.F.\n2. Look only at the denominators and find their L.C.M.\n\nThen put the first result over the second result.\n\nDo not mix the numerator numbers with the denominator numbers.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "hcf_intuition"
        }
      ]
    },
    "hcf_intuition": {
      "id": "hcf_intuition",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Think of the formula as two independent lanes.\n\nTop lane → numerators → H.C.F.\nBottom lane → denominators → L.C.M.\n\nOnly after both lanes are solved do we combine the answers into one fraction.\n\nThis is exactly the structure used in the chapter's worked example.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "hcf_example_setup"
        }
      ]
    },
    "hcf_example_setup": {
      "id": "hcf_example_setup",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Worked example from the chapter:\n\nFind the H.C.F. of:\n\n2/3, 8/9, 16/81 and 10/27\n\nStep 1: Take only the numerators:\n2, 8, 16, 10\n\nTheir H.C.F. is 2.\n\nStep 2: Take only the denominators:\n3, 9, 81, 27\n\nTheir L.C.M. is 81.\n\nNow put the two results together:\n\nH.C.F. = 2/81\n\nThe important part is not the arithmetic alone. It is choosing H.C.F. for the numerators and L.C.M. for the denominators.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "hcf_check"
        }
      ]
    },
