    "hcf_formula_check_remedy": {
      "id": "hcf_formula_check_remedy",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's remember the formula using the two-lane pattern:\n\nNumerators → H.C.F.\nDenominators → L.C.M.\n\nSo the final form must be:\n\nH.C.F.(a,c,e) / L.C.M.(b,d,f)\n\nThe other combinations swap or repeat the operations incorrectly.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "hcf_more_numbers"
        }
      ]
    },
    "hcf_more_numbers": {
      "id": "hcf_more_numbers",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The rule does not stop at three fractions. It works the same way for any number of fractions presented in the problem.\n\nFor example, with five fractions:\n\na/b, c/d, e/f, g/h and i/j\n\nYou still do only two calculations:\n\nH.C.F. of a, c, e, g, i\n\nand\n\nL.C.M. of b, d, f, h, j\n\nThen place the first result over the second.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lcm_formula"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lcm_formula": {
      "id": "lcm_formula",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Now turn the rule around.\n\nFor fractions a/b, c/d and e/f:\n\nL.C.M. = L.C.M.(a, c, e) / H.C.F.(b, d, f)\n\nSo:\n\n1. Find the L.C.M. of all numerators.\n2. Find the H.C.F. of all denominators.\n3. Put the numerator result over the denominator result.\n\nThis is the exact opposite pairing from the H.C.F. formula.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lcm_intuition"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lcm_intuition": {
      "id": "lcm_intuition",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A quick way to remember both formulas is to focus on what happens on each side.\n\nFor H.C.F.:\nTop = H.C.F.\nBottom = L.C.M.\n\nFor L.C.M.:\nTop = L.C.M.\nBottom = H.C.F.\n\nThe operation on the numerator and denominator always switches together when you move from H.C.F. to L.C.M.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lcm_example_one"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lcm_example_one": {
      "id": "lcm_example_one",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The chapter's objective solutions include this type of direct calculation:\n\nL.C.M. of 1/3, 5/6, 2/9 and 4/27\n\nFirst, take numerators:\n1, 5, 2, 4\n\nTheir L.C.M. is 20.\n\nNow take denominators:\n3, 6, 9, 27\n\nTheir H.C.F. is 3.\n\nTherefore:\n\nL.C.M. = 20/3\n\nThe important step is keeping the numerator operation and denominator operation in the correct order.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "lcm_check"
        }
      ]
    },
